<p><strong>Seeking Greenlights:</strong> Yes</p>
<p><strong>Page Type:</strong> SCP Article</p>
<p><strong>Page Layout:</strong> The story will primarily focus on research logs from researchers trying to learn about Threadmother.</p>
<p><strong>Elevator Pitch:</strong> A 8000 years old rudimentary AI constructed of threads, strings and drums proves to be quite a puzzle for researchers. Only one researcher manages to connect with the AI when all others have failed.</p>
<p><strong>Central Narrative:</strong> Threadmother is an ancient entity. Even in the oldest known writing, the ancient texts from Sumer, the entity is mentioned. Ancient man had developed a religious ritual wherein they would cut leather straps and strings and drape them across a huge cave in what is today Jordan. The strings were attached to simple drums, animal hides strung just above the floor with wooden pegs. On the drums there was either sand or small pebbles. As the strings were connected to the drums, pulling a string would cause the sand and pebbles to bounce and dance, and the ancients would believe that reading the sand and pebbles allowed them to foresee the future.</p>
<p>The narrative is constructed as logs of video interviews of the different researches who worked on the project to examine Threadmother. Every second log will be with Francesca Boutin, a researcher with a doctorate in musical theory. Francesca Boutin is the only one who has managed to actually communicate with Threadmother, but her results haven't been reproduced and the Foundation is trying to figure out what exactly she's doing to communicate with Threadmother.</p>
<p>Threadmother can only sense vibrations, not words, not sight, not anything else. Francesca comes from a troubled childhood. She would sit in her room, listening to her parents fight and argue on the other side of the door. When her dad dies, it would just be her mother, rambling and crying. When her mother started dating again, it would be fighting and arguing again. Francescas whole childhood was experienced in much the same was as Threadmother experiences the world, mumbling voices where the words can't really be distinguished.</p>
<p>&quot;All of you keep calling her SCP-XXXX. I'm the only one that calls her by her name, and I'm the one who've made progress. That should give you cause for self-reflection, but I know that it won't&quot;.</p>

