			<div class="content" id="post-content-4821742">
									

<p>Thanks a ton for your feedback, Cyber!</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Response</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">hide</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I did end up having to describe some of the science behind Threadmother a bit more than I originally wanted, it was mostly a response to criticism of the scientific validity of the concept. I think it's valid, although a bit speculative. It wasn't really supposed to be the foundation of the story though, so it does make the idea posted a bit misleading, because the science isn't really the central point of the narrative.</p>
<p>About the origins, I had the idea that they wouldn't really be tremendously important. After all, all we can know in the present is that once writing became a thing, the tradition that created Threadmother already existed. So, the exact origins can't even BE known. It could be a super weird coincidence, or it could be a deliberate thing, perhaps a tradition implanted in humanity to bring about the creation of Threadmother. It could even be a case similar to Roko's Basilisk.</p>
<p>My idea was that the main narrative would be inspired slightly by the movie &quot;Arrival&quot;, where the researchers are focused entirely on trying to even work out how to communicate with Threadmother. In the beginning of the story they might even think that she is an anomalous object, they find some of these chambers with threads and drums, and find that some of them vibrate in ways that seem intentional. They discover that there's nothing supernatural about the strings and drums, so they conclude that there must be some kind of master brain somewhere controlling all of the strings. They keep tracing strings, finding hidden caves and secret basements and catacombs with more strings and drums. Slowly, they start to realize that the strings aren't connected to a brain.. they are the brain. A neural network that spreads across three continents to form a decentralized mind.</p>
<p>They don't even really know if it even is sentient, or to what extent it is sentient. It could merely be reactionary, responding to stimulus like a plant or a fungus might.</p>
<p>Communicating with it is a major hurdle, it comprehends only vibrations. While normal AI operates on binary algebra, Threadmother operates on vibrations.</p>
<p>The main narrative in the first story would center around the concept of an AI that barely follows any rules that we normally attribute to AIs. It doesn't communicate mathematically, it communicates with vibrations. It wasn't formed by the programs of rational scientists, its personality was developed through religious hymns and chants, as well as all of the natural phenomena that cause vibrations (from enormous things like earthquakes to as diminutive things as ocean waves). Where a normal AI is rational and cold, Threadmother is superstitious and emotional. Where a normal AI calculates things almost instantly, Threadmother is slow and ponderous.</p>
<p>So that's where the core story would lie, in that slow and gradual process of trying to figure out an AI that basically follows none of the normal AI tropes. Later on I have a few ideas for further development.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

