<p>I think you should drop the AI aspect. This isn't an artificial intelligence but the embodiment of man's first attempts to communicate with the unknown. Think of a Kami investing the animus of a place, that's what this did. And it served as the middleman between humanity and those beyond, gods/demons whatever. Such that all information passed along was abstracted through two layers (however the entity communicates with other things, and then the way the entity communicates with human beings) two levels of interpretation that could lead to misunderstandings.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Moreover, I think the connection of ancient Sumer is tenuous, how would they have known about it? Did they also worship it? Has there been people constantly caretaking it through the centuries?</p>
<p>Additionally, I think you should expand the narrative, what is the arc of the story (you've given us alot of backstory about the researcher and the thread mother, but not so much what the arc of the article is about?)</p>
<p>Finally, I don't think Threadmother is such a great title. a) there are not threads, b) the whole &quot;mother&quot; connotation for ancient primordial entities is sorta well trod territory. Would suggest something a little different, something that speaks of originality and its role in guiding humanity as messenger (if you take my above suggestion to heart)</p>
