<p>First of all, a tremendous heap of thanks for the feedback, Monseur Karpin.</p>
<p>I'm definitely going to remove the AI aspect, you're not the first to have a hurdle with it, and as mentioned, it isn't benefitting the story really.</p>
<p>How does a simpler name sound, like &quot;Spinner&quot; or &quot;The Spinner&quot;?</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>About the connection to ancient Sumer, the Threadmother is vaguely mentioned or hinted at in a number of religions of Europe and the Middle-East, first of which is Sumer. My idea is that a lot of existing religions have a small and secret cult within them that knows about Threadmother and works to preserve and spread her. Whenever a new religion formed, the cult worked to imbed itself in the new religion. The notions of threads and strands of fate found in a number of religions is the working of the cult. It isn't a necessarily evil or sinister cult, I don't want the story to be that simple.</p>
<p>About the arc of the story, my thought is this: The logs switch between one log from a researcher in a specific field (starting maybe with a Historian, maybe an Acoustic Engineer, etc.), and one log with Francesca. The switching between normal researcher and Francesca serves two purposes, it gives an excuse to add some background information on Threadmother, and it provides a contrast to how Francesca approaches the SCP, and how she manages to achieve results. She starts out slightly aggressive, thinking her research is being put into question. The Foundation has struggled to replicate her achievements with the phenomena, and Francesca feels that her entire methodology and approach is being put into question. She warms up a bit to the interviewer, and shares her process.</p>
<p>She starts to talk a little bit about her childhood, and how Threadmother perceives the world in a way similar to how she did. She hid in her room, behind a closed door, hearing arguments and fights on the other side, but being unable to hear the words. That's how Threadmother senses the world, the vibrations and the tone, but not the words. She later discovers how Threadmother uses a sort of names to distinguish between people, and imbedded in those names is her view on that person. Someone she likes will have a name that consists of very pleasing sounds, while people she dislikes will have names that sound very dissonant. Threadmother communicates in a way where every &quot;word&quot; also has an imbedded feeling, depending on how dissonant or not the sound is.</p>
<p>The arc is the slow exploration of Threadmother and her way of communicating, and even the way in which she connects to Francesca. Many of the events in Francesca life will have mirrors of them in the life of Threadmother. Francesca heard violent fights and arguments as a child, just like Threadmother heard the screaming and jostling of war around her, but without seeing the events directly. They're both scarred, they've got temper, they've got some anger, but they aren't bad or evil.</p>
</div>
